      "id": 1,
      "text": "Midlothian grew around coal mining; the Richmond and Danville Railroad built Coalfield Station in 1850, later renamed Midlothian.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        8,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midlothian%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from early 18th‑century Wooldridge coal mines and echoes Scottish Lothian, named by brothers from East and West Lothian.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        11,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midlothian%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first Black church in Chesterfield County, First Baptist Church of Midlothian, was founded in 1846, making it a historic landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midlothian%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the Revolutionary War, coal from Midlothian powered the cannon factory at Westham, supplying shot for the Continental Army.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
